# What this port was made from, and how to notice when it moves.
#
# Source:  dsh-crew 0.7.0  (https://github.com/stuarthu/dsh-crew)
# Tag:     v0.7.0
# Commit:  87a4332293bf3f5a0720a3a90bf58cba2b1120fb
#
# Every line below is the SHA-256 of a dsh-crew file AS COMMITTED at that tag —
# the state this port was made from. One line per ported file: the eight role
# prompts, the five host/*.js files whose rules this port carries or skips,
# principles.md and README.md. Fifteen lines. ADR 0005 says why not more, and
# the last comment block in this file says what is left out and why.
#
# To see what changed since, clone the newest TAG into a throwaway folder:
#
#   TMP=$(mktemp -d)
#   git clone --quiet https://github.com/stuarthu/dsh-crew "$TMP/dsh-crew"
#   git -C "$TMP/dsh-crew" checkout --quiet <the newest tag>
#   cd "$TMP/dsh-crew" && sha256sum -c ~/workspace/claude-crew/upstream.sums
#   cd "$TMP/dsh-crew" && shasum -a 256 -c ~/workspace/claude-crew/upstream.sums   # macOS
#
# A tag, not `main`, and not somebody's working copy: a tag is a decision a
# person made, and this port's 0.2.0 pass compared against a mid-flight commit,
# which is how the gap grew to 11,000 lines before anyone measured it.
# NEVER read, write or run anything in ~/workspace/dsh-crew. That is the user's
# own working copy, it is usually half-finished, and a sum taken from it is
# worthless. `sha256sum -c` compares the WORKING TREE of whatever checkout you
# stand in, so a fresh clone at a tag is the only state that means anything; if
# you reuse a checkout anyway, run `git status` in it first and read the answer.
#
# A line reported as FAILED means that dsh-crew file changed. The comment above
# it says which claude-crew file to look at, and why. porting.md holds the full
# map and the steps of a port pass.
#
# BEFORE YOU READ THE DIFF. Ten paragraphs in this port say something
# deliberately DIFFERENT from upstream: eight places where upstream contradicts
# itself, numbered 1 to 8, and two gaps neither project had, numbered 9 and 10.
# Nine of the fifteen lines below carry the word DIVERGENCE in their comment: all
# eight roles/*.md files and principles.md. Entries 9 and 10 both reach EVERY
# role prompt, which is why nine lines carry them and not four. For those, a
# FAILED line may mean "we decided otherwise, on the record" rather than "we are
# behind". Open
# porting.md's deliberate divergence table FIRST and work through its rows for
# that file: upstream may have fixed the same thing its own way, and then you
# take their wording and delete the row. Skip that step and you either copy an
# upstream defect back in or delete a fix of ours as though it were a missed
# port. The table is the whole record and it stands alone — no document
# outside this repository is needed, or exists.
#
# After carrying a change across, replace that line with the new sum:
#   cd "$TMP/dsh-crew" && sha256sum roles/pm.md
# and update the Source, Tag and Commit lines above when a whole pass is done.
#
# `sha256sum -c` cannot report a file it has never heard of, so a NEW upstream
# role, or a rule moved into a new file, is invisible here. porting.md carries
# the by-hand check for that.

# roles/pm.md  ->  skills/team-lane/SKILL.md
